// Ordering a list by an order the caller chooses.
//
// The division of labour is the whole design, and it needs no explaining:
//
//   xs.sort()              the element type's OWN order, built in, using the
//                          `cmp` method the comparison operators already use
//   sort.by(xs, order)     an order you hand it
//
// One is the natural order of a type, one is a chosen order, and there is
// exactly one spelling of each. Nothing here duplicates `xs.sort()`; a
// program that wants a type's own order writes that and this module is not
// involved.
//
// An `order` is an `Order<T>`, which is an ordinary one-method interface --
// there is no function type in this language (docs/closures-decision.md).
// So all three of these are the same call, and the third is what the first
// two abbreviate:
//
//   sort.by(ps, by_x);                              a function's name
//   sort.by(ps, (Point a, Point b) => a.x - b.x);   a lambda
//   sort.by(ps, ByAxis(true));                      an object with fields
//
// Everything below is written in this language, over `size()`, `[i]`,
// `[i] =` and `push`. It asks the runtime for nothing: the comparison is an
// ordinary interface call, and the merge is ordinary code.
//
// **A `List`, not an `Array`.** Every parameter type here is written, and
// there is no overloading and no type that covers both, so covering arrays
// would mean a second name for each of these four -- eight public names for
// four operations. `xs.sort()` already orders an `Array` by the element
// type's own `cmp`, which is the common case; a chosen order over an array
// waits until a program needs one badly enough to justify the names.

/// A total order over `T`: negative if `a` comes first, zero if neither
/// does, positive if `b` comes first.
///
/// **`Order`, not `Less` or `Comparator`.** It is a total order, not a
/// predicate, and `cmp` answers three ways. `Less` would invite a `bool`,
/// which is the single most common mistake in every language with a
/// comparison callback.
///
/// **The method is `cmp`, which §6.2 already means.** A type's natural order
/// is `int T.cmp(T other)` -- a receiver and one parameter -- and an order
/// imposed from outside is `int cmp(T a, T b)` -- two parameters, no
/// receiver. Different shapes, so different dispatch slots and no collision;
/// one name, one meaning, two receivers. Negative, zero or positive in both,
/// so a hand-written order and the built-in one can never disagree about
/// what a comparison returns.
pub interface Order<T> {
    int cmp(T a, T b);
}

/// Sort `xs` in place, by `order`. **Stable**: elements `order` calls equal
/// keep the order they were in, so sorting by one key and then by another is
/// the ordinary way to get a compound order, and `sort.by` is safe to use
/// that way. `xs.sort()` promises the same thing, and the two must not
/// differ in a property a program builds on.
///
/// A bottom-up merge sort: O(n log n) comparisons always, one scratch list,
/// no recursion and so no stack depth to worry about on a long list. It is
/// the same algorithm `rt_sort_with` runs for `xs.sort()`, written here
/// instead of there, and it needs nothing new in the runtime: the scratch
/// list is a `List<T>` and the comparison is an interface call.
///
/// **What `order.cmp` may do.** It may read `xs`. Changing `xs` from inside
/// it is a mistake, and it is the one difference from `xs.sort()`, which
/// marks the list and traps: this cannot mark anything, because marking is
/// a runtime operation and there is no primitive for it -- deliberately, as
/// adding one to the seam (docs/stdlib-seam.md) to catch a caller's bug
/// would be a poor trade. What happens instead is bounded and safe: a
/// shorter list makes an index trap, a longer one leaves the new elements
/// unsorted. Memory is never at risk, because every access here is a checked
/// `[i]`.
pub void by<T>(List<T> xs, Order<T> order) {
    int n = xs.size();
    if (n < 2) {
        return;
    }
    // The scratch half of the merge. Filled with the list's own elements so
    // that it has `n` slots to write into; what is in them does not matter.
    List<T> tmp = [];
    for (T x in xs) {
        tmp.push(x);
    }
    int width = 1;
    while (width < n) {
        int lo = 0;
        while (lo < n) {
            int mid = lo + width;
            int hi = lo + 2 * width;
            if (mid >= n) {
                // The tail is a run of its own and is already sorted.
                break;
            }
            if (hi > n) {
                hi = n;
            }
            int i = lo;
            int j = mid;
            int k = lo;
            while (i < mid && j < hi) {
                // `<= 0` takes from the LEFT run on a tie, which is the
                // whole of what makes this stable.
                if (order.cmp(xs[i], xs[j]) <= 0) {
                    tmp[k] = xs[i];
                    i = i + 1;
                } else {
                    tmp[k] = xs[j];
                    j = j + 1;
                }
                k = k + 1;
            }
            while (i < mid) {
                tmp[k] = xs[i];
                i = i + 1;
                k = k + 1;
            }
            while (j < hi) {
                tmp[k] = xs[j];
                j = j + 1;
                k = k + 1;
            }
            for (int back in lo .. hi) {
                xs[back] = tmp[back];
            }
            lo = lo + 2 * width;
        }
        width = width * 2;
    }
}

/// The greatest element of `xs` under `order`.
///
/// `max(xs, o)` is the element `by(xs, o)` would leave LAST, ties included,
/// and `min` is the one it would leave first. That identity is the reason
/// each keeps the element it does when several compare equal -- `max` the
/// last of them, `min` the first -- and it is worth more than a "first wins"
/// rule that would make `max` and a sort disagree about which of two equal
/// elements a program gets.
///
/// One pass, n-1 comparisons, nothing allocated.
///
/// **An empty list traps.** There is no element to return and no honest way
/// to invent one; returning an `Option` instead would put a `match` at every
/// call site to handle a case most callers have already ruled out. It is the
/// same answer `xs[0]` gives to the same question, with the same reasoning.
pub T max<T>(List<T> xs, Order<T> order) {
    int n = xs.size();
    if (n == 0) {
        trap("sort.max: the list is empty, so there is no greatest element");
    }
    // The best INDEX, not the best element. A `T best` local would be the
    // ordinary way to write this, and it is refused when `T` turns out to be
    // a type the calling module keeps private: a local's type is checked for
    // visibility after monomorphisation has substituted it, and this module
    // cannot name that type. An index needs no such local, and it costs a
    // retain less per improvement besides.
    int best = 0;
    for (int i in 1 .. n) {
        // `>= 0`: a later element that ties replaces the earlier one, so
        // this is the element a stable sort would leave last.
        if (order.cmp(xs[i], xs[best]) >= 0) {
            best = i;
        }
    }
    return xs[best];
}

/// The least element of `xs` under `order`: the element `by(xs, order)`
/// would leave FIRST. An empty list traps, as `max` does.
pub T min<T>(List<T> xs, Order<T> order) {
    int n = xs.size();
    if (n == 0) {
        trap("sort.min: the list is empty, so there is no least element");
    }
    int best = 0;
    for (int i in 1 .. n) {
        // `< 0` only: a tie leaves the earlier element in place.
        if (order.cmp(xs[i], xs[best]) < 0) {
            best = i;
        }
    }
    return xs[best];
}

/// Where `want` is in `xs`, which must already be sorted by `order`.
///
/// **`search` is `index_of` for a sorted list, and answers the same question
/// with the same type.** `xs.index_of(v)` gives `Option<int>` -- the FIRST
/// index holding it, or `None` -- and so does this, in O(log n) comparisons
/// instead of n. Two functions that answer "where is this value" must not
/// disagree, so this one finds the first of a run of equal elements too,
/// which costs one comparison more than stopping at any match and is what
/// makes `search` and `index_of` interchangeable on a sorted list.
///
/// **It is not an insertion point.** Java's `binarySearch` returns
/// `-(insertion point) - 1` when the value is absent, which is two answers
/// in one integer and a sign convention every caller has to remember;
/// C#'s does the same. "Where would this go" is a different question from
/// "where is this", and if a program ever needs it, it earns a name of its
/// own rather than a second meaning for this one's result.
///
/// `order` must be the order `xs` is sorted by. If it is not, the answer is
/// unhelpful but never unsafe: every access here is a checked `[i]`.
pub Option<int> search<T>(List<T> xs, T want, Order<T> order) {
    // Lower bound: the first index whose element does not come before
    // `want`. The halving is `lo + (hi - lo) / 2`, not `(lo + hi) / 2`,
    // which cannot overflow however long the list is.
    int lo = 0;
    int hi = xs.size();
    while (lo < hi) {
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (order.cmp(xs[mid], want) < 0) {
            lo = mid + 1;
        } else {
            hi = mid;
        }
    }
    if (lo < xs.size() && order.cmp(xs[lo], want) == 0) {
        return Option<int>.Some(lo);
    }
    return Option<int>.None;
}
